  ✕ Nil map for missing key
   ╭─[test.lis:5:12]
 4 │ fn test(outer: Map<string, Bag>) {
 5 │   let _b = outer["x"]
   ·            ─────┬────
   ·                 ╰── `Bag` contains `mut Map<string, int>`, which is nil when the key is missing
 6 │ }
   ╰────
  help: Use `outer.get(key)`. Bracket reads return a zero value when the key is missing, and the zero value of `Bag` contains a nil `mut Map<string, int>`, which panics on write, so this bracket read is disallowed · code: [infer.map_read_no_zero]
